r gain, the AC system has to blow air that’s *much* colder than 65°F, often in the 40s. If you then switch the AC off but leave the fan on the same speed, you’re suddenly getting a breeze that’s 65°F instead of 45°F. That’s a huge drop in convective cooling. In those circumstances, I’d bet you a free car wash that the two settings would feel starkly different, even if the cabin temperature sensor eventually reads 65°F in both cases. **So maybe the rule isn’t “65 always feels different”—it’s “a climate control system’s setpoint number is a poor proxy for comfort because your body senses multiple heat flows, and sometimes those flows happen to line up by coincidence.”** Your experience is the coincidence. Next time you’re driving in blazing sun and high humidity, if you’re curious, try the side-by-side: let the AC stabilize at 65°F, feel it, then turn off the AC but leave the temperature set at 65°F and the fan on the same speed. After a few minutes, the air temperature might still hover near 65, but I suspect you’ll notice a difference for the first time. Or not—and if not, then you’re either super lucky with your car’s design or you’re just a thermodynamic unicorn, and I respect that.
